{
  "dom_id": "step-q263155",
  "name": "Question 15",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13956659/assets/752774e8849a8dfbb2badb1124b4be72f585751d7051eddb57d64c471990ec08.png)\n\nGiven that the points $(4,5)$ and $(2,-3)$ lie on a diameter of a circle, what are the center and radius of the circle?\n\nThe circle is centered at  $(${{field-1}}$,${{field-2}}$)$ and its radius is{{field-3}}.",
  "worked_solution": "The center of the circle is located at the midpoint of the diameter. So, we can calculate the coordinates of the center using the midpoint formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(h,k) & =(\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2},\\frac{{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{4+2}{2},\\frac{5+(-3)}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{6}{2},\\frac{2}{2}) \\\\  & =(3,1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe radius can be found by calculating the distance between the center $(3,1)$ and a point on the circle, such as $(4,5):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{({x}_{1}-{x}_{2})}^{2}+{({y}_{1}-{y}_{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(3-4)}^{2}+{(1-5)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(-4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+16} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{17}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the circle is centered at $(3,1),$ and its radius is $\\sqrt{17}.$",
